===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# {{&lit|en|play|on}}
#: {{ux|en|I knew I was losing, but I '''played on''' anyways.}}
# {{q|also 'play upon'}} To [[exploit]] (a potential weakness or vulnerability) by [[exacerbate|exacerbating]]. 
#: ''to '''play on''' someone's fears''
# {{q|also 'play upon'}} To exploit (a double meaning, similarity in sound, etc.) for humorous or creative effect.
#: ''This joke '''plays on''' the fact that 'thirsty' sounds similar to 'Thursday'.''
#  {{q|also 'play upon'}} To impinge upon in a recurring or nagging way.
#: ''to '''play on''' one's mind, or conscience''
#  {{q|also 'play upon'}} To frequently allude to or return to as a theme.
#: ''The pamphlet '''plays on''' the idea that all social conflict is class-based.''
# {{lb|en|cricket|of a [[batsman]]}} to [[hit]] the [[ball]] into his own [[wicket]], thus being [[out]] [[bowled]]
# {{lb|en|sports}} To let the game continue after a [[foul]] has been committed, because the situation is advantageous to the team who would be awarded a foul.
# {{lb|en|sports|_|such as soccer}} To cause (an opposing player) to be [[onside]] rather than [[offside]] due to one's position on the field of play.
#: ''The ref said that Smith was offside, but I think that Jones '''played''' him '''on'''.''

